# The build context is FOUR FILES — two binaries per supported target.
# Everything else is excluded.
#
# Without this, `docker build` uploads the whole working tree to the
# daemon before running the first step — and `target/` alone is ~11 GB
# during normal development. It never reached the image (the Dockerfile
# copies two paths), but every image build paid for shipping it, and in
# the 2 GB colima VM a context that size is also a way to run the disk
# out from under the cluster.
#
# The binaries are cross-compiled on the HOST by scripts/build-images.sh
# (static musl); nothing compiles in Docker, so nothing else belongs in the
# context. Two targets are readmitted because the two clusters differ:
# aarch64 for the colima dev cluster on the Mac, x86_64 for the homelab
# nodes. The Dockerfile's TARGET build-arg selects which one is copied.
#
# Exclude-all-then-readmit is deliberate: a new directory in the repo is
# excluded by default, and a build that needs something new has to say so
# here. The opposite default silently grows the context again.
*

!target/aarch64-unknown-linux-musl/release/regnmed-api
!target/aarch64-unknown-linux-musl/release/regnmed
!target/x86_64-unknown-linux-musl/release/regnmed-api
!target/x86_64-unknown-linux-musl/release/regnmed
